#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186520487 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "454a250f-2e0e-5eff-ad08-2e1a4569c3b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2x-1}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520091} {:answer/id #uuid "4cf36f6a-ac30-5d76-9499-88f79530ef58" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{2x-1}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520108} {:answer/id #uuid "a63c2527-a87a-5317-bb52-435b49980a68" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2x-1}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520218} {:answer/id #uuid "ceae46e6-5020-51b1-a35e-422b64ddda99" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4\\sqrt{2x-1}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520281} {:answer/id #uuid "edcc6c31-a212-5340-8bdb-e1b0f8c22746" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{4\\sqrt{2x-1}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520341}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186520091} :answer-field/id #uuid "7e463a5a-0bf7-5c15-8a84-e8fb513aab4c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520404}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "98cde390-f268-5ffd-9f2f-31f016a0c432" :question/math-academy-id "q-107303" :question/problem "Given that $y=\\sqrt{2x-1},$ find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.$" :question/worked-solution "We use the chain rule in the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nLet $u=2x-1.$ Then $y=\\sqrt{u}={u}^{1/2}.$ Differentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}=\\frac{1}{2}{u}^{-1/2}=\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{u}}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=2.\n$$\n\nMultiplying the two together gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{u}}\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{u}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2x-1}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186520516 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "33c7c493-bcdb-5703-aa16-7951eec23ed2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520068} {:answer/id #uuid "961a1fb5-7dbb-4d31-93ea-cbe5abcbdc15" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{4x+1}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186576511}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186520068} :answer-field/id #uuid "3b694189-fec1-5a06-8456-a524e215acf6" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520382}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "fa666076-05a3-50c7-be3d-b7a39d6d4f55" :question/math-academy-id "q-314192" :question/problem "The slope of the tangent to the curve $y=\\sqrt{4x+1}$ at the point where $x=0$ is{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "To find the slope at a given point, we have to find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ at that point.\n\nWe use the chain rule in the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nLet $u=4x+1.$ Then $y=\\sqrt{u}.$ Differentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}=\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{u}},\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=4.\n$$\n\nMultiplying the two together gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{u}}\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{2\\sqrt{4x+1}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting $x=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{x=0} & ={\\left(\\frac{4}{2\\sqrt{4x+1}}\\right)|}_{x=0} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{2\\sqrt{4(0)+1}} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186524983 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bc8d190d-9c67-5acd-8583-f14a36220ea6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524684}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186524684} :answer-field/id #uuid "f05471f2-c115-5266-86b4-2c4bebd70c45" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524945}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "45936e93-e188-5489-9cc9-795dad15e0d6" :question/math-academy-id "q-342792" :question/problem "The slope of the tangent to the curve $y=\\sqrt{3{x}^{2}+2x}$ at the point where $x=-1$ is{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "To find the slope at a given point, we have to find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ at that point.\n\nWe use the chain rule in the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nLet $u=3{x}^{2}+2x.$ Then $y=\\sqrt{u}.$ Differentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}=\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{u}},\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=6x+2.\n$$\n\nMultiplying the two together gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{u}}\\cdot (6x+2) \\\\  & =\\frac{6x+2}{2\\sqrt{3{x}^{2}+2x}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting $x=-1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{x=-1} & ={\\left(\\frac{6x+2}{2\\sqrt{3{x}^{2}+2x}}\\right)|}_{x=-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{6(-1)+2}{2\\sqrt{3(-1)^{2}+2(-1)}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-4}{2\\sqrt{1}} \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186524994 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ff3851f0-019b-54f0-9d24-526c6e29abe2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-30\\ln(5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524839}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186524839} :answer-field/id #uuid "d83b75c0-fe56-5390-8126-bd377ed6af9e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524934}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "70b1c8a4-0636-5508-ab80-4e4e15ec81e5" :question/math-academy-id "q-340970" :question/problem "Find the slope $m$ of the tangent to the curve $y=2\\cdot {5}^{1-3x}$ at the point where $x=0.$\n\n$m=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we express $y$ as the composite function\n\n$$\ny=2\\cdot {5}^{u},\\,u=1-3x.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}=2\\cdot {5}^{u}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-3.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =2\\cdot {5}^{u}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)\\cdot (-3) \\\\  & =-6\\cdot {5}^{1-3x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to find the slope $m$ of the tangent to the curve at $x=0$, we have to evaluate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ at that point as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m={\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{x=0} & ={(-6\\cdot {5}^{1-3x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡5)|}_{x=0} \\\\  & =-6\\cdot {5}^{1-3(0)}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5) \\\\  & =-6\\cdot 5\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5) \\\\  & =-30\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186524996 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ca0e728f-63f5-5bf9-9222-2a283f84a906" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{14}{e^2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524710}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186524710} :answer-field/id #uuid "11e1ae92-136f-5d68-8353-b8a4c9e485ce" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524848}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "7798a8be-b1f8-549d-9dbb-a2ddea411006" :question/math-academy-id "q-340954" :question/problem "Find the slope $m$ of the tangent to the curve $y={e}^{-2{x}^{7}}$ at the point where $x=1.$\n\n$m=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we express $y$ as the composite function\n\n$$\ny={e}^{u},\\,u=-2{x}^{7}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}={e}^{u},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-14{x}^{6}.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{u}\\cdot (-14{x}^{6}) \\\\  & =-14{x}^{6}{e}^{u} \\\\  & =-14{x}^{6}{e}^{-2{x}^{7}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to find the slope $m$ of the tangent to the curve at $x=1$, we have to evaluate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ at that point as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m={\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{x=1} & ={(-14{x}^{6}{e}^{-2{x}^{7}})|}_{x=1} \\\\  & =-14(1)^{6}{e}^{-2(1)^{7}} \\\\  & =-14{e}^{-2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186524997 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "207209c4-3cd7-5f9a-8f55-b6d3c323a8ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)\\,x+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)+\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524441} {:answer/id #uuid "47a3f504-906f-5250-a397-60c7605afb97" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)\\,x+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)+\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524506} {:answer/id #uuid "8d428376-e2b0-53e2-b314-797b7522cd0c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)\\,x-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)+\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524605} {:answer/id #uuid "a77b2a43-bd2d-5fe7-a7da-2538cecd54fa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)\\,x-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)+\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524650} {:answer/id #uuid "e12ce1c7-49ac-5fd0-9ccc-f097ef76e6f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)\\,x+\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)+\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524774}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186524441} :answer-field/id #uuid "717c0cdc-ae39-5590-87a2-a75a93baebfc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524890}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "77c551c0-c155-56eb-9f11-d8d660941796" :question/math-academy-id "q-107463" :question/problem "Find the equation of the tangent to the curve $y={4}^{2x+1}$ at the point where $x=-1.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we express $y$ as the composite function\n\n$$\ny={4}^{u},\\,u=2x+1.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}={4}^{u}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=2.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={4}^{u}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4)\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =2\\cdot {4}^{2x+1}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to find the slope of the tangent to the curve at $x=-1$, we have to evaluate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ at that point. \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{x=-1} & ={(2\\cdot {4}^{2x+1}\\operatorname{ln}⁡4)|}_{x=-1} \\\\  & =2\\cdot {4}^{-1}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡((4)^{1/2}) \\\\  & =ln\\,(\\,\\sqrt{4}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAlso, the $y$-value of the point on the curve where $x={x}_{1}=-1$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & ={({4}^{2x+1})|}_{x=-1} \\\\  & ={4}^{2(-1)+1} \\\\  & ={4}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we find the equation of the tangent at that point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-\\frac{1}{4} & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)\\cdot (x+1) \\\\ y-\\frac{1}{4} & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)\\,x+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2) \\\\ y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)\\,x+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)+\\frac{1}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186525021 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2e53a687-845e-5e38-b650-cd7d2e960b1e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "192" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524465} {:answer/id #uuid "4c87e60d-6195-503b-b8b0-645b9615d373" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "384" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524511} {:answer/id #uuid "92b0203b-17bf-5bcc-b180-500fc847f62a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3,072" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524613} {:answer/id #uuid "ca95a77f-004b-588b-8c88-365d7dedd455" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "768" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524712} {:answer/id #uuid "de1da834-e484-5065-82b1-ac2bfe5f309a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "758" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524761}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186524511} :answer-field/id #uuid "4a15449d-4ef5-5450-8663-fb7daf5af1c5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524872}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "a24feddb-3c6c-5b5d-be09-ecb6ef41d845" :question/math-academy-id "q-48622" :question/problem "Given that $f(x)=(3{x}^{2}-10)^{4},$ find the slope at the point $(2,16).$" :question/worked-solution "To find the slope at a given point, we have to find $\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}$ at that point.\n\nWe use the chain rule to find $\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}.$\n\nFirst, we express $f(x)$ as a composite function:\n\n$$\nf={g}^{4},\\,g=3{x}^{2}-10\n$$\n\nThe chain rule states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}g}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}g}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}g}=4{g}^{3},\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}g}{\\text{d}x}=6x.\n$$\n\nMultiplying the two together and substituting for $g$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =4{g}^{3}\\cdot (6x) \\\\  & =24x(3{x}^{2}-10)^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the tangent line at the point where $x=2$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}|}_{x=2} & ={24x{(3{x}^{2}-10)}^{3}|}_{x=2} \\\\  & =24(2){(3(2)^{2}-10)}^{3} \\\\  & =384.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186529418 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "136b59f4-a9e4-5d8c-b869-dad46630033a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "13\\,\\text{min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529422} {:answer/id #uuid "461e25d2-998f-5231-9ad9-048327e6eb31" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "10\\,\\text{min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529433} {:answer/id #uuid "8ce962ca-5f25-5bd0-8fc8-ea3116721951" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "24\\,\\text{min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529450} {:answer/id #uuid "90c189d5-a421-5b5e-906b-c9e5138b97ec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8\\,\\text{min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529452} {:answer/id #uuid "9ab3b094-2f4a-5213-b0de-26f0f8cd39f7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "11\\,\\text{min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529455}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186529422} :answer-field/id #uuid "95c3cd81-387f-52f6-8598-4e98964b059c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529453}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "08518a50-98e2-5d9c-a6f5-59dc1a4a2277" :question/problem "A heated metal body cools in a room at ${18}^{∘}\\text{C}.$ Its temperature is ${60}^{∘}\\text{C}$ after $3$ minutes and ${39}^{∘}\\text{C}$ after $7$ minutes. The temperature $T(t)$, in degrees Celsius, obeys Newton's law of cooling, with $t$ measured in minutes since cooling began. To the nearest minute, how long after cooling began does the body reach ${25}^{∘}\\text{C}$?" :question/retired true :question/worked-solution "Newton's law of cooling states that the rate at which the temperature $T(t)$ of a body changes is proportional to the difference between its temperature and its surroundings. Therefore, we have the inhibited decay equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dT}{dt}=-k(T-18),\\qquad k>0.\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate our differential equation by separating the variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dT}{T-18} & =-k\\,dt \\\\ \\int \\frac{dT}{T-18} & =-k\\int dt \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|T-18| & =-kt+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this is the solution to an inhibited decay equation, we have $T-18>0$ for all $t>0.$\nNext, we solve for $T(t),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T-18 & ={e}^{-kt+C} \\\\ T-18 & ={e}^{C}{e}^{-kt} \\\\ T-18 & =K{e}^{-kt} \\\\ T & =18+K{e}^{-kt}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $K={e}^{C}$ is a constant of integration. \n\nApply the two observed conditions $T(3)=60$ and $T(7)=39$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}60 & =18+Ke^{-3k} \\\\ 42 & =Ke^{-3k} \\\\ 39 & =18+Ke^{-7k} \\\\ 21 & =Ke^{-7k}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDivide the excess temperatures to eliminate $K$, then take natural logarithms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{21}{42} & =\\frac{Ke^{-7k}}{Ke^{-3k}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{2} & =e^{-4k} \\\\ \\ln\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right) & =-4k \\\\ k & =\\frac{\\ln{\\left(2 \\right)}}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow substitute this rate into $T(3)=60$ to determine the constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}42 & =Ke^{-3\\left(\\frac{\\ln{\\left(2 \\right)}}{4}\\right)} \\\\ K & =42e^{3\\left(\\frac{\\ln{\\left(2 \\right)}}{4}\\right)} \\\\ K & =42\\left(\\frac{42}{21}\\right)^{\\frac{3}{4}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution satisfying both observations is\n\n$$\nT(t)=18+42\\left(\\frac{42}{21}\\right)^{\\frac{3}{4}}e^{-\\left(\\frac{\\ln{\\left(2 \\right)}}{4}\\right)t}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the two observation times confirms the model:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(3) & =18+(42\\left(\\frac{42}{21}\\right)^{\\frac{3}{4}})e^{-\\left(\\frac{\\ln{\\left(2 \\right)}}{4}\\right)\\cdot 3}=60 \\\\ T(7) & =18+(42\\left(\\frac{42}{21}\\right)^{\\frac{3}{4}})e^{-\\left(\\frac{\\ln{\\left(2 \\right)}}{4}\\right)\\cdot 7}=39\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, let ${t}_{1}$ be the time when the body reaches ${25}^{∘}\\text{C}.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T({t}_{1}) & =25 \\\\ 18+Ke^{-k{t}_{1}} & =25 \\\\ Ke^{-k{t}_{1}} & =7 \\\\ e^{-k{t}_{1}} & =\\frac{7}{K} \\\\ -k{t}_{1} & =\\ln\\left(\\frac{7}{K}\\right) \\\\ {t}_{1} & =-\\frac{1}{k}\\ln\\left(\\frac{7}{K}\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstitute the values of $K$ and $k$ to obtain the time measured since cooling began:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}_{1} & =-\\frac{1}{\\frac{\\ln{\\left(2 \\right)}}{4}}\\ln\\left(\\frac{7}{42\\left(\\frac{42}{21}\\right)^{\\frac{3}{4}}}\\right) \\\\ {t}_{1} & =3+4\\frac{\\ln\\left(\\frac{1}{6}\\right)}{\\ln\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}≈13\\,\\text{min}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186529434 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2061e414-b4ea-5c57-ad60-fc9a5c241449" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "15\\,\\text{min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529425} {:answer/id #uuid "3148407a-a1b7-5be3-ae1b-0b7c3252c208" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "19\\,\\text{min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529429} {:answer/id #uuid "642227aa-f6b5-5f2c-9789-1258fcc2941b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "18\\,\\text{min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529439} {:answer/id #uuid "81dae93e-910d-5d6a-839a-efb7b838187c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "36\\,\\text{min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529447} {:answer/id #uuid "84a07af3-e9fe-57c9-9b86-f82e9bbbe6bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "10\\,\\text{min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529449}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186529429} :answer-field/id #uuid "ab2a34ec-9222-55be-9af3-842e7b4fe46d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529457}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "55811d49-71e0-57b3-9d4a-d1c8d9d4f58f" :question/problem "A heated metal body cools in a room at ${12}^{∘}\\text{C}.$ Its temperature is ${64}^{∘}\\text{C}$ after $1$ minute and ${49}^{∘}\\text{C}$ after $4$ minutes. The temperature $T(t)$, in degrees Celsius, obeys Newton's law of cooling, with $t$ measured in minutes since cooling began. To the nearest minute, how long after cooling began does the body reach ${19}^{∘}\\text{C}$?" :question/retired true :question/worked-solution "Newton's law of cooling states that the rate at which the temperature $T(t)$ of a body changes is proportional to the difference between its temperature and its surroundings. Therefore, we have the inhibited decay equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dT}{dt}=-k(T-12),\\qquad k>0.\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate our differential equation by separating the variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dT}{T-12} & =-k\\,dt \\\\ \\int \\frac{dT}{T-12} & =-k\\int dt \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|T-12| & =-kt+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this is the solution to an inhibited decay equation, we have $T-12>0$ for all $t>0.$\nNext, we solve for $T(t),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T-12 & ={e}^{-kt+C} \\\\ T-12 & ={e}^{C}{e}^{-kt} \\\\ T-12 & =K{e}^{-kt} \\\\ T & =12+K{e}^{-kt}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $K={e}^{C}$ is a constant of integration. \n\nApply the two observed conditions $T(1)=64$ and $T(4)=49$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}64 & =12+Ke^{-1k} \\\\ 52 & =Ke^{-1k} \\\\ 49 & =12+Ke^{-4k} \\\\ 37 & =Ke^{-4k}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDivide the excess temperatures to eliminate $K$, then take natural logarithms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{37}{52} & =\\frac{Ke^{-4k}}{Ke^{-1k}} \\\\ \\frac{37}{52} & =e^{-3k} \\\\ \\ln\\left(\\frac{37}{52}\\right) & =-3k \\\\ k & =- \\frac{\\ln{\\left(\\frac{37}{52} \\right)}}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow substitute this rate into $T(1)=64$ to determine the constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}52 & =Ke^{-1\\left(- \\frac{\\ln{\\left(\\frac{37}{52} \\right)}}{3}\\right)} \\\\ K & =52e^{1\\left(- \\frac{\\ln{\\left(\\frac{37}{52} \\right)}}{3}\\right)} \\\\ K & =52\\left(\\frac{52}{37}\\right)^{\\frac{1}{3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution satisfying both observations is\n\n$$\nT(t)=12+52\\left(\\frac{52}{37}\\right)^{\\frac{1}{3}}e^{-\\left(- \\frac{\\ln{\\left(\\frac{37}{52} \\right)}}{3}\\right)t}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the two observation times confirms the model:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(1) & =12+(52\\left(\\frac{52}{37}\\right)^{\\frac{1}{3}})e^{-\\left(- \\frac{\\ln{\\left(\\frac{37}{52} \\right)}}{3}\\right)\\cdot 1}=64 \\\\ T(4) & =12+(52\\left(\\frac{52}{37}\\right)^{\\frac{1}{3}})e^{-\\left(- \\frac{\\ln{\\left(\\frac{37}{52} \\right)}}{3}\\right)\\cdot 4}=49\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, let ${t}_{1}$ be the time when the body reaches ${19}^{∘}\\text{C}.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T({t}_{1}) & =19 \\\\ 12+Ke^{-k{t}_{1}} & =19 \\\\ Ke^{-k{t}_{1}} & =7 \\\\ e^{-k{t}_{1}} & =\\frac{7}{K} \\\\ -k{t}_{1} & =\\ln\\left(\\frac{7}{K}\\right) \\\\ {t}_{1} & =-\\frac{1}{k}\\ln\\left(\\frac{7}{K}\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstitute the values of $K$ and $k$ to obtain the time measured since cooling began:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}_{1} & =-\\frac{1}{- \\frac{\\ln{\\left(\\frac{37}{52} \\right)}}{3}}\\ln\\left(\\frac{7}{52\\left(\\frac{52}{37}\\right)^{\\frac{1}{3}}}\\right) \\\\ {t}_{1} & =1+3\\frac{\\ln\\left(\\frac{7}{52}\\right)}{\\ln\\left(\\frac{37}{52}\\right)}≈19\\,\\text{min}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186529436 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "035a9a44-7a16-5905-a0dc-77a5358cf6b1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "18\\,\\text{min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529417} {:answer/id #uuid "09046438-fbef-58e9-b053-5d1dfaf0a2ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "30\\,\\tex