& ={e}^{-kt+C} \\\\ T-23 & ={e}^{C}{e}^{-kt} \\\\ T-23 & =K{e}^{-kt} \\\\ T & =23+K{e}^{-kt}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $K={e}^{C}$ is a constant of integration. \n\nApply the two observed conditions $T(2)=110$ and $T(8)=62$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}110 & =23+Ke^{-2k} \\\\ 87 & =Ke^{-2k} \\\\ 62 & =23+Ke^{-8k} \\\\ 39 & =Ke^{-8k}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDivide the excess temperatures to eliminate $K$, then take natural logarithms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{39}{87} & =\\frac{Ke^{-8k}}{Ke^{-2k}} \\\\ \\frac{13}{29} & =e^{-6k} \\\\ \\ln\\left(\\frac{13}{29}\\right) & =-6k \\\\ k & =- \\frac{\\ln{\\left(\\frac{13}{29} \\right)}}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow substitute this rate into $T(2)=110$ to determine the constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}87 & =Ke^{-2\\left(- \\frac{\\ln{\\left(\\frac{13}{29} \\right)}}{6}\\right)} \\\\ K & =87e^{2\\left(- \\frac{\\ln{\\left(\\frac{13}{29} \\right)}}{6}\\right)} \\\\ K & =87\\left(\\frac{87}{39}\\right)^{\\frac{1}{3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution satisfying both observations is\n\n$$\nT(t)=23+87\\left(\\frac{87}{39}\\right)^{\\frac{1}{3}}e^{-\\left(- \\frac{\\ln{\\left(\\frac{13}{29} \\right)}}{6}\\right)t}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the two observation times confirms the model:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(2) & =23+(87\\left(\\frac{87}{39}\\right)^{\\frac{1}{3}})e^{-\\left(- \\frac{\\ln{\\left(\\frac{13}{29} \\right)}}{6}\\right)\\cdot 2}=110 \\\\ T(8) & =23+(87\\left(\\frac{87}{39}\\right)^{\\frac{1}{3}})e^{-\\left(- \\frac{\\ln{\\left(\\frac{13}{29} \\right)}}{6}\\right)\\cdot 8}=62\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, let ${t}_{1}$ be the time when the body reaches ${25}^{∘}\\text{C}.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T({t}_{1}) & =25 \\\\ 23+Ke^{-k{t}_{1}} & =25 \\\\ Ke^{-k{t}_{1}} & =2 \\\\ e^{-k{t}_{1}} & =\\frac{2}{K} \\\\ -k{t}_{1} & =\\ln\\left(\\frac{2}{K}\\right) \\\\ {t}_{1} & =-\\frac{1}{k}\\ln\\left(\\frac{2}{K}\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstitute the values of $K$ and $k$ to obtain the time measured since cooling began:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}_{1} & =-\\frac{1}{- \\frac{\\ln{\\left(\\frac{13}{29} \\right)}}{6}}\\ln\\left(\\frac{2}{87\\left(\\frac{87}{39}\\right)^{\\frac{1}{3}}}\\right) \\\\ {t}_{1} & =2+6\\frac{\\ln\\left(\\frac{2}{87}\\right)}{\\ln\\left(\\frac{13}{29}\\right)}≈30\\,\\text{min}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186529438 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "108ed220-ff0d-5a3e-9732-871db25e264d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "42\\,\\text{min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529421} {:answer/id #uuid "36b98352-a45c-5756-ba26-e8f13b324484" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "22\\,\\text{min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529430} {:answer/id #uuid "40afc4ac-32fd-5122-8320-a1b94bc430b1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "20\\,\\text{min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529431} {:answer/id #uuid "64fd9fd9-a113-5826-9e9f-9bf1153c8645" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "19\\,\\text{min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529440} {:answer/id #uuid "751594c1-eba1-5f1c-a604-af02b2e24eff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "12\\,\\text{min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529444}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186529430} :answer-field/id #uuid "f54cefd1-735e-592f-a832-09bdfef2567e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529463}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "6178d66f-8125-5a9f-a46c-197579fd4dfe" :question/problem "A heated metal body cools in a room at ${14}^{∘}\\text{C}.$ Its temperature is ${114}^{∘}\\text{C}$ after $2$ minutes and ${79}^{∘}\\text{C}$ after $7$ minutes. The temperature $T(t)$, in degrees Celsius, obeys Newton's law of cooling, with $t$ measured in minutes since cooling began. To the nearest minute, how long after cooling began does the body reach ${32}^{∘}\\text{C}$?" :question/retired true :question/worked-solution "Newton's law of cooling states that the rate at which the temperature $T(t)$ of a body changes is proportional to the difference between its temperature and its surroundings. Therefore, we have the inhibited decay equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dT}{dt}=-k(T-14),\\qquad k>0.\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate our differential equation by separating the variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dT}{T-14} & =-k\\,dt \\\\ \\int \\frac{dT}{T-14} & =-k\\int dt \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|T-14| & =-kt+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this is the solution to an inhibited decay equation, we have $T-14>0$ for all $t>0.$\nNext, we solve for $T(t),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T-14 & ={e}^{-kt+C} \\\\ T-14 & ={e}^{C}{e}^{-kt} \\\\ T-14 & =K{e}^{-kt} \\\\ T & =14+K{e}^{-kt}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $K={e}^{C}$ is a constant of integration. \n\nApply the two observed conditions $T(2)=114$ and $T(7)=79$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}114 & =14+Ke^{-2k} \\\\ 100 & =Ke^{-2k} \\\\ 79 & =14+Ke^{-7k} \\\\ 65 & =Ke^{-7k}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDivide the excess temperatures to eliminate $K$, then take natural logarithms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{65}{100} & =\\frac{Ke^{-7k}}{Ke^{-2k}} \\\\ \\frac{13}{20} & =e^{-5k} \\\\ \\ln\\left(\\frac{13}{20}\\right) & =-5k \\\\ k & =- \\frac{\\ln{\\left(\\frac{13}{20} \\right)}}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow substitute this rate into $T(2)=114$ to determine the constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}100 & =Ke^{-2\\left(- \\frac{\\ln{\\left(\\frac{13}{20} \\right)}}{5}\\right)} \\\\ K & =100e^{2\\left(- \\frac{\\ln{\\left(\\frac{13}{20} \\right)}}{5}\\right)} \\\\ K & =100\\left(\\frac{100}{65}\\right)^{\\frac{2}{5}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution satisfying both observations is\n\n$$\nT(t)=14+100\\left(\\frac{100}{65}\\right)^{\\frac{2}{5}}e^{-\\left(- \\frac{\\ln{\\left(\\frac{13}{20} \\right)}}{5}\\right)t}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the two observation times confirms the model:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(2) & =14+(100\\left(\\frac{100}{65}\\right)^{\\frac{2}{5}})e^{-\\left(- \\frac{\\ln{\\left(\\frac{13}{20} \\right)}}{5}\\right)\\cdot 2}=114 \\\\ T(7) & =14+(100\\left(\\frac{100}{65}\\right)^{\\frac{2}{5}})e^{-\\left(- \\frac{\\ln{\\left(\\frac{13}{20} \\right)}}{5}\\right)\\cdot 7}=79\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, let ${t}_{1}$ be the time when the body reaches ${32}^{∘}\\text{C}.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T({t}_{1}) & =32 \\\\ 14+Ke^{-k{t}_{1}} & =32 \\\\ Ke^{-k{t}_{1}} & =18 \\\\ e^{-k{t}_{1}} & =\\frac{18}{K} \\\\ -k{t}_{1} & =\\ln\\left(\\frac{18}{K}\\right) \\\\ {t}_{1} & =-\\frac{1}{k}\\ln\\left(\\frac{18}{K}\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstitute the values of $K$ and $k$ to obtain the time measured since cooling began:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}_{1} & =-\\frac{1}{- \\frac{\\ln{\\left(\\frac{13}{20} \\right)}}{5}}\\ln\\left(\\frac{18}{100\\left(\\frac{100}{65}\\right)^{\\frac{2}{5}}}\\right) \\\\ {t}_{1} & =2+5\\frac{\\ln\\left(\\frac{9}{50}\\right)}{\\ln\\left(\\frac{13}{20}\\right)}≈22\\,\\text{min}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186529446 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "59b59f9f-0328-5e3a-b3a0-9ae94cd9029b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "9\\,\\text{min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529437} {:answer/id #uuid "6cc12c7b-7d04-5703-959a-c62329b886f5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "11\\,\\text{min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529442} {:answer/id #uuid "8ed0441f-d7c8-59ac-b964-78a874544707" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "13\\,\\text{min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529451} {:answer/id #uuid "b6c8bae0-5d37-5021-8cbd-592d9fbcffea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6\\,\\text{min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529459} {:answer/id #uuid "c9905343-f98e-5172-a713-e8ad676df9ec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "26\\,\\text{min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529461}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186529451} :answer-field/id #uuid "56bac0d5-d078-5477-b2fe-4ca3e2469c85" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529435}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "7bca55c5-9e2a-5abf-bde2-2e93390e2953" :question/problem "Simon measures the temperature of the tea in his cup, and the thermometer displays a reading of ${59}^{∘}C.$ After $2$ minutes, Simon repeats the measurement and the thermometer displays a reading of  ${51}^{∘}C.$ To the nearest minute, how long does it take for the tea to decrease its temperature to ${26}^{∘}C$ if the temperature in the room is ${12}^{∘}C?$ \n\n*Hint: You may assume that the temperature $T(t)$ of the tea in Simon's cup decreases at a rate that obeys Newton's law of cooling, where $T$ is measured in degrees Celsius and $t>0$ is the time, in minutes, since the first measurement.*" :question/retired true :question/worked-solution "Newton's law of cooling states that the rate at which the temperature $T(t)$ of a body changes is proportional to the difference between its temperature and its surroundings. Therefore, we have the inhibited decay equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-k(T-{T}_{S}),\n$$\n\nwhere ${T}_{S}$ is the temperature of the surroundings, and $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality.\n\nIn our case, ${T}_{S}={12}^{∘}\\text{C},$ and we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-k(T-12).\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate our differential equation by separating the variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dT}{T-12} & =-k\\,dt \\\\ \\int \\frac{dT}{T-12} & =-k\\int dt \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|T-12| & =-kt+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this is the solution to an inhibited decay equation, we have $T-12>0$ for all $t>0.$\nNext, we solve for $T(t),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T-12 & ={e}^{-kt+C} \\\\ T-12 & ={e}^{C}{e}^{-kt} \\\\ T-12 & =K{e}^{-kt} \\\\ T & =12+K{e}^{-kt}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $K={e}^{C}$ is a constant of integration. \n\nWe're told that the initial temperature reading was ${59}^{∘}C.$ So, we apply the initial condition $T(0)=59,$ and solve for $K:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}59 & =12+K{e}^{0} \\\\ 59 & =12+K \\\\ K & =47\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following expression for $T(t):$\n\n$$\nT(t)=12+47{e}^{-kt}\n$$\n\nWe're also told that, after $2$ minutes, the thermometer reading is ${51}^{∘}C.$ So, applying the condition $T(2)=51,$ we can solve for $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}51 & =12+47{e}^{-2k} \\\\ 39 & =47{e}^{-2k} \\\\ \\frac{39}{47} & ={e}^{-2k} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{39}{47}\\right) & =-2k \\\\ k & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{39}{47}\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, let ${t}_{1}$ be the time when the temperature of the tea is ${26}^{∘}C.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T({t}_{1}) & =26 \\\\ 12+47{e}^{-k{t}_{1}} & =26 \\\\ 47{e}^{-k{t}_{1}} & =14 \\\\ {e}^{-k{t}_{1}} & =\\frac{14}{47} \\\\ -k{t}_{1} & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{14}{47}\\right) \\\\ {t}_{1} & =-\\frac{1}{k}\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{14}{47}\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting in our value for $k,$ we conclude that the tea will have a temperature of ${26}^{∘}C$ after\n\n$$\n{t}_{1}=\\frac{-\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{14}{47}\\right)}{-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{39}{47}\\right)}≈13\\,\\text{min},\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest minute."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186529448 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "14340294-f6e6-5843-a6fe-4ec754aa2b3a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "15\\,\\text{min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529423} {:answer/id #uuid "26c1b56e-8363-5b7a-9cd7-fc35a89b4352" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "30\\,\\text{min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529428} {:answer/id #uuid "42a5f854-c421-5906-9531-dfb338eb3d00" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8\\,\\text{min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529432} {:answer/id #uuid "68bd5f21-2fc4-57db-ab21-cd5920a10ff8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "14\\,\\text{min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529441} {:answer/id #uuid "b750d51c-f2a0-5a6c-a1e6-ddae4ed1f957" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "11\\,\\text{min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529460}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186529423} :answer-field/id #uuid "773705ba-e6bc-585e-ac23-61ef9313aea0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529445}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "83a0fd6e-0040-59af-810e-aa17184cf1d7" :question/problem "The water boils in a pan at a temperature of ${100}^{∘}C.$ The pan with water is then placed to a room with a temperature of ${17}^{∘}C,$ and the temperature of water decreases. After $3$ minutes, it cools to ${83}^{∘}C.$ To the nearest minute, how long does it take for the water to have a temperature of ${43}^{∘}C?$ \n\n*Hint: You may assume that the temperature reading $T(t)$ of the water in the pan decreases at a rate that obeys Newton's law of cooling, where $T$ is measured in degrees Celsius and $t>0$ is the time, in minutes, since the water starts cooling.*" :question/retired true :question/worked-solution "Newton's law of cooling states that the rate at which the temperature $T(t)$ of a body changes is proportional to the difference between its temperature and its surroundings. Therefore, we have the inhibited decay equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-k(T-{T}_{S}),\n$$\n\nwhere ${T}_{S}$ is the temperature of the surroundings, and $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality.\n\nIn our case, ${T}_{S}={17}^{∘}\\text{C},$ and we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-k(T-17).\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate our differential equation by separating the variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dT}{T-17} & =-k\\,dt \\\\ \\int \\frac{dT}{T-17} & =-k\\int dt \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|T-17| & =-kt+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this is the solution to an inhibited decay equation, we have $T-17>0$ for all $t>0.$\nNext, we solve for $T(t),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T-17 & ={e}^{-kt+C} \\\\ T-17 & ={e}^{C}{e}^{-kt} \\\\ T-17 & =K{e}^{-kt} \\\\ T & =17+K{e}^{-kt}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $K={e}^{C}$ is a constant of integration. \n\nWe're told that the initial temperature was ${100}^{∘}C.$ So, we apply the initial condition $T(0)=100,$ and solve for $K:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}100 & =17+K{e}^{0} \\\\ 100 & =17+K \\\\ K & =83\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following expression for $T(t):$\n\n$$\nT(t)=17+83{e}^{-kt}\n$$\n\nWe're also told that, after $3$ minutes, it cools to ${83}^{∘}C.$ So, applying the condition $T(3)=83,$ we can solve for $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}83 & =17+83{e}^{-3k} \\\\ 66 & =83{e}^{-3k} \\\\ \\frac{66}{83} & ={e}^{-3k} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{66}{83}\\right) & =-3k \\\\ k & =-\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{66}{83}\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, let ${t}_{1}$ be the time when the water has a temperature of ${43}^{∘}C.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T({t}_{1}) & =43 \\\\ 17+83{e}^{-k{t}_{1}} & =43 \\\\ 83{e}^{-k{t}_{1}} & =26 \\\\ {e}^{-k{t}_{1}} & =\\frac{26}{83} \\\\ -k{t}_{1} & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{26}{83}\\right) \\\\ {t}_{1} & =-\\frac{1}{k}\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{26}{83}\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting in our value for $k,$ we conclude that the temperature of water will be ${43}^{∘}C$ after\n\n$$\n{t}_{1}=\\frac{-\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{26}{83}\\right)}{-\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{66}{83}\\right)}≈15\\,\\text{min},\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest minute."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186529464 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0db77da5-938c-5d21-b72a-0f710e5c2a0b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "15\\,\\text{min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529420} {:answer/id #uuid "1cb8c333-7a33-50bf-877e-90ce08332112" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "10\\,\\text{min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529424} {:answer/id #uuid "9a43cb5e-136c-5d82-8309-2ec9f8922e29" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "14\\,\\text{min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529454} {:answer/id #uuid "9efca7bd-9559-5f53-b339-6146e7712ff6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "32\\,\\text{min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529456} {:answer/id #uuid "f372127d-3efe-5b72-a30e-572b7ee9abc2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "17\\,\\text{min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529462}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186529462} :answer-field/id #uuid "b4ae2599-3eaa-5407-bab7-c011910aa3c1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529458}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "fa306e16-1691-5350-96e5-61b0e9060130" :question/problem "A heated metal body cools in a room at ${22}^{∘}\\text{C}.$ Its temperature is ${82}^{∘}\\text{C}$ after $2$ minutes and ${54}^{∘}\\text{C}$ after $7$ minutes. The temperature $T(t)$, in degrees Celsius, obeys Newton's law of cooling, with $t$ measured in minutes since cooling began. To the nearest minute, how long after cooling began does the body reach ${31}^{∘}\\text{C}$?" :question/retired true :question/worked-solution "Newton's law of cooling states that the rate at which the temperature $T(t)$ of a body changes is proportional to the difference between its temperature and its surroundings. Therefore, we have the inhibited decay equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dT}{dt}=-k(T-22),\\qquad k>0.\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate our differential equation by separating the variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dT}{T-22} & =-k\\,dt \\\\ \\int \\frac{dT}{T-22} & =-k\\int dt \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|T-22| & =-kt+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this is the solution to an inhibited decay equation, we have $T-22>0$ for all $t>0.$\nNext, we solve for $T(t),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T-22 & ={e}^{-kt+C} \\\\ T-22 & ={e}^{C}{e}^{-kt} \\\\ T-22 & =K{e}^{-kt} \\\\ T & =22+K{e}^{-kt}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $K={e}^{C}$ is a constant of integration. \n\nApply the two observed conditions $T(2)=82$ and $T(7)=54$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}82 & =22+Ke^{-2k} \\\\ 60 & =Ke^{-2k} \\\\ 54 & =22+Ke^{-7k} \\\\ 32 & =Ke^{-7k}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDivide the excess temperatures to eliminate $K$, then take natural logarithms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{32}{60} & =\\frac{Ke^{-7k}}{Ke^{-2k}} \\\\ \\frac{8}{15} & =e^{-5k} \\\\ \\ln\\left(\\frac{8}{15}\\right) & =-5k \\\\ k & =- \\frac{\\ln{\\left(\\frac{8}{15} \\right)}}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow substitute this rate into $T(2)=82$ to determine the constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}60 & =Ke^{-2\\left(- \\frac{\\ln{\\left(\\frac{8}{15} \\right)}}{5}\\right)} \\\\ K & =60e^{2\\left(- \\frac{\\ln{\\left(\\frac{8}{15} \\right)}}{5}\\right)} \\\\ K & =60\\left(\\frac{60}{32}\\right)^{\\frac{2}{5}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution satisfying both observations is\n\n$$\nT(t)=22+60\\left(\\frac{60}{32}\\right)^{\\frac{2}{5}}e^{-\\left(- \\frac{\\ln{\\left(\\frac{8}{15} \\right)}}{5}\\right)t}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the two observation times confirms the model:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(2) & =22+(60\\left(\\frac{60}{32}\\right)^{\\frac{2}{5}})e^{-\\left(- \\frac{\\ln{\\left(\\frac{8}{15} \\right)}}{5}\\right)\\cdot 2}=82 \\\\ T(7) & =22+(60\\left(\\frac{60}{32}\\right)^{\\frac{2}{5}})e^{-\\left(- \\frac{\\ln{\\left(\\frac{8}{15} \\right)}}{5}\\right)\\cdot 7}=54\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, let ${t}_{1}$ be the time when the body reaches ${31}^{∘}\\text{C}.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T({t}_{1}) & =31 \\\\ 22+Ke^{-k{t}_{1}} & =31 \\\\ Ke^{-k{t}_{1}} & =9 \\\\ e^{-k{t}_{1}} & =\\frac{9}{K} \\\\ -k{t}_{1} & =\\ln\\left(\\frac{9}{K}\\right) \\\\ {t}_{1} & =-\\frac{1}{k}\\ln\\left(\\frac{9}{K}\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstitute the values of $K$ and $k$ to obtain the time measured since cooling began:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}_{1} & =-\\frac{1}{- \\frac{\\ln{\\left(\\frac{8}{15} \\right)}}{5}}\\ln\\left(\\frac{9}{60\\left(\\frac{60}{32}\\right)^{\\frac{2}{5}}}\\right) \\\\ {t}_{1} & =2+5\\frac{\\ln\\left(\\frac{3}{20}\\right)}{\\ln\\left(\\frac{8}{15}\\right)}≈17\\,\\text{min}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186532969 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0137e495-9519-563c-a9ee-6f5584f3dcad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{e}^{-4y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}={e}^{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532968} {:answer/id #uuid "0bea7869-a91a-5392-b25d-ed17806c1610" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{e}^{x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}={e}^{4y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532974} {:answer/id #uuid "42b0ce1a-0757-53db-93de-7de1b53bce5a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{e}^{-x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}={e}^{4y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532983} {:answer/id #uuid "5e22d1ff-80c3-5392-a495-c399a03b3ab0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{e}^{-x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}={e}^{-4y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532988} {:answer/id #uuid "89981f02-ad8a-513e-828a-e400b88e66d3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{e}^{4y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}={e}^{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532992}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186532968} :answer-field/id #uuid "0a0a0640-dade-51e4-9d76-499e8b522693" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532971}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "03ab52b6-83e6-54a5-afb6-55184c5bbcb6" :question/problem "Separate the variables in the following differential equation: \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}={e}^{x+4y}\n$$\n\nWork on a domain where all displayed expressions are defined." :question/worked-solution "A differential equation is *separable* if it can be written in the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=h(x)g(y).\n$$\n\nIf an equation is separable, we say that the variables are *separated* when it is written in the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{g(y)}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=h(x).\n$$\n\nNotice that our differential equation is separable since it can be written as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}={e}^{x}\\cdot {e}^{4y}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we separate the variables as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{{e}^{4y}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}={e}^{x},\n$$\n\nor, equivalently,\n\n$$\n{e}^{-4y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}={e}^{x}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186532991 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "707a9662-a514-5dc4-bc0a-687d138a9a25" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3x+2}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{5x}{4+{y}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532989} {:answer/id #uuid "7fdac19f-bf7b-5a5c-9d62-d0171d74c4d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(3x+2)\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{5x}{4+{y}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532990} {:answer/id #uuid "a486f8f5-5d40-591c-b231-a3bd909c081a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(4+{y}^{2})\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{5x}{3x+2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532997} {:answer/id #uuid "c247858e-5af0-5089-b95b-c8d1999ef52c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4+{y}^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{5x}{3x+2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533002} {:answer/id #uuid "d3623e5b-41cb-51d2-908e-c2825e724307" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(4+{y}^{2})\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{3x+2}{5x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533005}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186532997} :answer-field/id #uuid "08436a20-ac16-5134-98bd-17ac2d5fefc1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532970}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "81f64b39-6f57-500d-a5c6-3fe05e3c018a" :question/problem "Separate the variables in the following differential equation: \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{5x}{12x+3x{y}^{2}+8+2{y}^{2}}\n$$\n\nWork on a domain where all displayed expressions are defined." :question/worked-solution "A differential equation is *separable* if it can be written in the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=h(x)g(y).\n$$\n\nIf an equation is separable, we say that the variables are *separated* when it is written in the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{g(y)}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=h(x).\n$$\n\nNotice that our differential equation is separable since it can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{5x}{(3x+2)(4+{y}^{2})} \\\\  & =\\frac{5x}{3x+2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{4+{y}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we separate the variables as follows:\n\n$$\n(4+{y}^{2})\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{5x}{3x+2}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186532993 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0a42831a-fc6c-505f-91db-624efe60589b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2x+2}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{x}{3+{y}^{4}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532972} {:answer/id #uuid "0b865beb-828e-5981-a26d-0adafc67630e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(2x+2)\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{x}{3+{y}^{4}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532973} {:answer/id #uuid "2d681c76-75f8-5a31-94d4-5eb6019d8fb6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(3+{y}^{4})\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{2x+2}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532981} {:answer/id #uuid "5b6814b3-8f36-5644-b687-0444e7abfc14" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3+{y}^{4}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{x}{2x+2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532987} {:answer/id #uuid "c4b26db0-5685-5fb2-ae27-fec1821c4f35" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(3+{y}^{4})\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{x}{2x+2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533003}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186533003} :answer-field/id #uuid "5a356b80-c6dc-5374-83ae-82cbe3679ce2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532986}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "8d7617a1-0d91-5635-aa5d-2a4959553ae0" :question/problem "Separate the variables in the following differential equation: \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{x}{6x+2x{y}^{4}+6+2{y}^{4}}\n$$\n\nWork on a domain where all displayed expressions are defined." :question/worked-solution "A differential equation is *separable* if it can be written in the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=h(x)g(y).\n$$\n\nIf an equation is separable, we say that the variables are *separated* when it is written in the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{g(y)}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=h(x).\n$$\n\nNotice that our differential equation is separable since it can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{x}{(2x+2)(3+{y}^{4})} \\\\  & =\\frac{x}{2x+2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{3+{y}^{4}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we separate the variables as follows:\n\n$$\n(3+{y}^{4})\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{x}{2x+2}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186532999 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0e7c3b77-c214-557e-a804-a347cce54372" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3+{y}^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{x}{3x+2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532975} {:answer/id #uuid "2346f555-930d-5070-98b7-5e1217159460" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(3+{y}^{2})\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{x}{3x+2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532980} {:answer/id #uuid "8e4c9bb6-7717-511c-8838-e3db42547443" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(3+{y}^{2})\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{3x+2}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532994} {:answer/id #uuid "d87ff72b-4395-583b-be61-bc1afd372800" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3x+2}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{x}{3+{y}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533006} {:answer/id #uuid "f3ff3107-a7d2-59db-8b09-67444edeaf28" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(3x+2)\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{x}{3+{y}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533008}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186532980} :answer-field/id #uuid "11a3a2ee-3214-5652-a1e8-c95275af79fc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532976}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "aa110f6f-e8a3-5f93-ae17-13ecc7c36e75" :question/problem "Separate the variables in the following differential equation: \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{x}{9x+3x{y}^{2}+6+2{y}^{2}}\n$$\n\nWork on a domain where all displayed expressions are defined." :question/worked-solution "A differential equation is *separable* if it can be written in the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=h(x)g(y).\n$$\n\nIf an equation is separable, we say that the variables are *separated* when it is written in the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{g(y)}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=h(x).\n$$\n\nNotice that our differential equation is separable since it can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{x}{(3x+2)(3+{y}^{2})} \\\\  & =\\frac{x}{3x+2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{3+{y}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we separate the variables as follows:\n\n$$\n(3+{y}^{2})\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{x}{3x+2}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186533007 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "11adaa5b-badb-5193-b9a8-c971ca877a10" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(2+{y}^{2})\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{3x+4}{5x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532977} {:answer/id #uuid "13e25510-f32e-5583-a11d-67f2557629f0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2+{y}^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{5x}{3x+4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532978} {:answer/id #uuid "32262977-c1c8-542a-b231-36f8b3b5fc4c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(3x+4)\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{5x}{2+{y}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532982} {:answer/id #uuid "90518027-c2da-59c1-9c70-ef4bd227b41b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3x+4}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{5x}{2+{y}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532995} {:answer/id #uuid "ba119bc6-c3f3-53de-9d41-a1e681290b79" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(2+{y}^{2})\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{5x}{3x+4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533001}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186533001} :answer-field/id #uuid "b9778fd8-3195-5e5f-8896-1f489905a54b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533000}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "dd63e6d3-9aec-5442-bf20-03fb4398eaba" :question/problem "Separate the variables in the following differential equation: \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{5x}{6x+3x{y}^{2}+8+4{y}^{2}}\n$$\n\nWork on a domain where all displayed expressions are defined." :question/worked-solution "A differential equation is *separable* if it can be written in the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=h(x)g(y).\n$$\n\nIf an equation is separable, we say that the variables are *separated* when it is written in the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{g(y)}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=h(x).\n$$\n\nNotice that our differential equation is separable since it can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{5x}{(3x+4)(2+{y}^{2})} \\\\  & =\\frac{5x}{3x+4}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2+{y}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we separate the variables as follows:\n\n$$\n(2+{y}^{2})\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{5x}{3x+4}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186533009 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1636c696-c883-5327-b1d1-95d5845e693a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2+{y}^{4}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{5x}{2x+4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532979} {:answer/id #uuid "4afec3df-d25f-5250-be9a-b8e235f33d66" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(2x+4)\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{5x}{2+{y}^{4}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532984} {:answer/id #uuid "50fd0c44-809b-56e8-bffa-64daf91f5728" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(2+{y}^{4})\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{2x+4}{5x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532985} {:answer/id #uuid "a0365396-629f-5676-a2ff-5f577f030cc8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2x+4}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{5x}{2+{y}^{4}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532996} {:answer/id #uuid "ccd8ba74-f3ec-5ca8-a446-60f1b1676907" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(2+{y}^{4})\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{5x}{2x+4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533004}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186533004} :answer-field/id #uuid "a98e2cd8-e387-5cb8-a456-9bec570be0f3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532998}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "f80f43dc-927c-5fe3-8a83-f47ed23baef3" :question/problem "Separate the variables in the following differential equation: \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{5x}{4x+2x{y}^{4}+8+4{y}^{4}}\n$$\n\nWork on a domain where all displayed expressions are defined." :question/worked-solution "A differential equation is *separable* if it can be written in the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=h(x)g(y).\n$$\n\nIf an equation is separable, we say that the variables are *separated* when it is written in the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{g(y)}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=h(x).\n$$\n\nNotice that our differential equation is separable since it can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{5x}{(2x+4)(2+{y}^{4})} \\\\  & =\\frac{5x}{2x+4}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2+{y}^{4}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we separate the variables as follows:\n\n$$\n(2+{y}^{4})\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{5x}{2x+4}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186534162 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bf4e16e0-23db-53b1-abb3-c9ad70be10d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "- \\frac{12}{e^{5}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534174}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186534174} :answer-field/id #uuid "34988f5d-3257-5d67-92f7-baf90f520a47" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534166}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "07e0afd0-4729-561c-85c0-8bd11d965ce5" :question/problem "Find the slope $m$ of the tangent to the curve $y=e^{-2{x}^{3}+3{x}^{2}-1}$ at the point where $x=2.$\n\n$m=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we express $y$ as the composite function\n\n$$\ny=e^{u},\\,u=-2{x}^{3}+3{x}^{2}-1.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}=e^{u},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-6{x}^{2}+6x.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\ & =e^{u}\\cdot (-6{x}^{2}+6x) \\\\ & =(-6{x}^{2}+6x)e^{u} \\\\ & =\\left(- 6 x^{2} + 6 x\\right) e^{- 2 x^{3} + 3 x^{2} - 1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to find the slope $m$ of the tangent to the curve at $x=2$, we have to evaluate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ at that point as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m=\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|_{x=2} & =(-6{x}^{2}+6x)e^{-2{x}^{3}+3{x}^{2}-1}|_{x=2} \\\\ & =(-6(2)^{2}+6(2))e^{-2(2)^{3}+3(2)^{2}-1} \\\\ & =-12\\cdot e^{-5} \\\\ & =- \\frac{12}{e^{5}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186534163 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dd4e95b0-b50d-508f-9ef8-253434a7e666" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{e}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534175}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186534175} :answer-field/id #uuid "ee820ac2-8d37-51d4-9ed8-671965aed8ce" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534176}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "0be68eb4-fee3-576f-ac3a-d663b1b8b84c" :question/problem "Find the slope $m$ of the tangent to the curve $y=e^{{x}^{3}-2{x}^{2}-1}$ at the point where $x=2.$\n\n$m=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we express $y$ as the composite function\n\n$$\ny=e^{u},\\,u={x}^{3}-2{x}^{2}-1.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}=e^{u},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=3{x}^{2}-4x.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\ & =e^{u}\\cdot (3{x}^{2}-4x) \\\\ & =(3{x}^{2}-4x)e^{u} \\\\ & =\\left(3 x^{2} - 4 x\\right) e^{x^{3} - 2 x^{2} - 1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to find the slope $m$ of the tangent to the curve at $x=2$, we have to evaluate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ at that point as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m=\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|_{x=2} & =(3{x}^{2}-4x)e^{{x}^{3}-2{x}^{2}-1}|_{x=2} \\\\ & =(3(2)^{2}-4(2))e^{(2)^{3}-2(2)^{2}-1} \\\\ & =4\\cdot e^{-1} \\\\ & =\\frac{4}{e}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186534167 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "232f798f-be1c-5feb-b192-a0aa8f6e1c00" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "- 4 e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534165}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186534165} :answer-field/id #uuid "8a6accfd-1e78-52a0-aedb-2b24f192df2d" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534169}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "3cb5cb4c-646e-54f0-9994-bb70583c546b" :question/problem "Find the slope $m$ of the tangent to the curve $y=e^{-{x}^{3}-2{x}^{2}+1}$ at the point where $x=-2.$\n\n$m=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we express $y$ as the composite function\n\n$$\ny=e^{u},\\,u=-{x}^{3}-2{x}^{2}+1.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}=e^{u},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-3{x}^{2}-4x.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\ & =e^{u}\\cdot (-3{x}^{2}-4x) \\\\ & =(-3{x}^{2}-4x)e^{u} \\\\ & =\\left(- 3 x^{2} - 4 x\\right) e^{- x^{3} - 2 x^{2} + 1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to find the slope $m$ of the tangent to the curve at $x=-2$, we have to evaluate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ at that point as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m=\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|_{x=-2} & =(-3{x}^{2}-4x)e^{-{x}^{3}-2{x}^{2}+1}|_{x=-2} \\\\ & =(-3(-2)^{2}-4(-2))e^{-(-2)^{3}-2(-2)^{2}+1} \\\\ & =-4\\cdot e^{1} \\\\ & =- 4 e.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186534168 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a58cd849-c476-5d2a-aa64-d8b6b04a580d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "20" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534172}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186534172} :answer-field/id #uuid "1f0c0cc9-ce45-5282-a4db-796430d6adf7" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534164}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "50ee9513-e646-50a4-ac90-7dd2ba8b0b7a" :question/problem "Find the slope $m$ of the tangent to the curve $y=e^{-{x}^{4}+3{x}^{2}+4}$ at the point where $x=-2.$\n\n$m=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we express $y$ as the composite function\n\n$$\ny=e^{u},\\,u=-{x}^{4}+3{x}^{2}+4.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}=e^{u},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-4{x}^{3}+6x.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\ & =e^{u}\\cdot (-4{x}^{3}+6x) \\\\ & =(-4{x}^{3}+6x)e^{u} \\\\ & =\\left(- 4 x^{3} + 6 x\\right) e^{- x^{4} + 3 x^{2} + 4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to find the slope $m$ of the tangent to the curve at $x=-2$, we have to evaluate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ at that point as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m=\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|_{x=-2} & =(-4{x}^{3}+6x)e^{-{x}^{4}+3{x}^{2}+4}|_{x=-2} \\\\ & =(-4(-2)^{3}+6(-2))e^{-(-2)^{4}+3(-2)^{2}+4} \\\\ & =20\\cdot e^{0} \\\\ & =20.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186534173 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a4527057-c6d9-5ff2-a509-e0ee92396e73" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "- \\frac{12}{e^{8}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534171}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186534171} :answer-field/id #uuid "91d46c5a-53e0-5eb2-af1a-36f86eae2ff0" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534170}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "a9053d53-838d-5e57-a71a-28c1ae22fb94" :question/problem "Find the slope $m$ of the tangent to the curve $y=e^{-2{x}^{3}+3{x}^{2}-4}$ at the point where $x=2.$\n\n$m=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we express $y$ as the composite function\n\n$$\ny=e^{u},\\,u=-2{x}^{3}+3{x}^{2}-4.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}=e^{u},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-6{x}^{2}+6x.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\ & =e^{u}\\cdot (-6{x}^{2}+6x) \\\\ & =(-6{x}^{2}+6x)e^{u} \\\\ & =\\left(- 6 x^{2} + 6 x\\right) e^{- 2 x^{3} + 3 x^{2} - 4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to find the slope $m$ of the tangent to the curve at $x=2$, we have to evaluate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ at that point as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m=\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|_{x=2} & =(-6{x}^{2}+6x)e^{-2{x}^{3}+3{x}^{2}-4}|_{x=2} \\\\ & =(-6(2)^{2}+6(2))e^{-2(2)^{3}+3(2)^{2}-4} \\\\ & =-12\\cdot e^{-8} \\\\ & =- \\frac{12}{e^{8}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537154 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "065744d3-2f5a-5333-b867-6a06c35d388d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}C}{\\text{d}t}=1(88-C)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537135} {:answer/id #uuid "0f0beb66-0276-51c0-85cb-0583ade2a515" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}C}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{88}{3}-C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537139} {:answer/id #uuid "ba2c7686-5b5d-54ba-8c3f-bea8a50d531f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}C}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{1}{3}(33-C)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537185} {:answer/id #uuid "c6661b18-4df7-5642-81c2-994f9a50f3fa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}C}{\\text{d}t}=3(88-C)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537187} {:answer/id #uuid "c99b67f5-dc3f-5e65-bc71-30e0b48c9bc0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}C}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{1}{3}(88-C)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537189}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537189} :answer-field/id #uuid "0af52cd0-6e16-5542-83a5-fea287479100" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537137}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "3f5ed13c-38a0-569e-996e-43867fd33621" :question/problem "A data center initially contains $33$ computers, and its maximum capacity is $88$ computers. Let $C(t)$ be the number of computers, where $t\\ge 0$ is the time in months. Its population obeys an inhibited growth model. The rate of increase equals $\\frac{1}{3}$ times the difference between the maximum capacity and the current population. Which differential equation models this population?" :question/worked-solution "The rate at which the number of computers $C(t)$ in the data center changes is proportional to the difference between the maximum capacity and the current number of computers. Therefore, we have the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}C}{\\text{d}t}=k(a-C),\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ is the maximum capacity, and $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality.\n\nIn our case, $a=88$ computers, and $k=\\frac{1}{3}$. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}C}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{1}{3}(88-C).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537159 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "15f8f887-00ef-5221-8ae5-98b89f49df18" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}N}{\\text{d}t}=922-N" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537140} {:answer/id #uuid "2f83f654-9fa4-5ea3-bea5-d401e167d4d7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}N}{\\text{d}t}=2(461-N)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537149} {:answer/id #uuid "41f9afda-dab8-5ea1-a543-78cd7ef01897" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}N}{\\text{d}t}=2(44-N)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537156} {:answer/id #uuid "6433720d-0437-5761-a06c-4f51d076ab3f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}N}{\\text{d}t}=-2(461-N)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537162} {:answer/id #uuid "fa19b207-aa8e-51a1-a052-84f0fcf25190" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}N}{\\text{d}t}=(461-N)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537201}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537149} :answer-field/id #uuid "7c7b268c-acbe-5154-8908-9ef519371ae4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537174}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "541cdb3d-4d10-57a0-803b-8e45225d32ba" :question/problem "A protected forest initially contains $44$ deer, and its maximum capacity is $461$ deer. Let $N(t)$ be the number of deer, where $t\\ge 0$ is the time in years. Its population obeys an inhibited growth model. The rate of increase equals $2$ times the difference between the maximum capacity and the current population. Which differential equation models this population?" :question/worked-solution "The rate at which the number of deer $N(t)$ in the protected forest changes is proportional to the difference between the maximum capacity and the current number of deer. Therefore, we have the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}N}{\\text{d}t}=k(a-N),\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ is the maximum capacity, and $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality.\n\nIn our case, $a=461$ deer, and $k=2$. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}N}{\\text{d}t}=2(461-N).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537163