ore, we have the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}C}{\\text{d}t}=k(a-C),\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ is the maximum capacity, and $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality.\n\nIn our case, $a=141$ computers, and $k=\\frac{2}{3}$. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}C}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{2}{3}(141-C).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537164 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2be091ba-6bb6-5883-8103-314cd3df15d4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}C}{\\text{d}t}=(88-C)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537148} {:answer/id #uuid "37e6c9ed-bf02-5ef5-8c5a-02b0b5c319c5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}C}{\\text{d}t}=-2(88-C)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537151} {:answer/id #uuid "4bd1d390-d8de-540e-9210-b415fa61cff9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}C}{\\text{d}t}=2(88-C)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537157} {:answer/id #uuid "563e8011-b5e6-5788-9e23-b206633aecb5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}C}{\\text{d}t}=2(33-C)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537160} {:answer/id #uuid "74d8e960-6bba-58e5-b08e-581702e7a76c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}C}{\\text{d}t}=176-C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537168}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537157} :answer-field/id #uuid "6211314d-6f8c-5806-9551-2e82c0125d2d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537161}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "6fe2e758-0b83-5e24-b0e9-a0f44f5d812a" :question/problem "A data center initially contains $33$ computers, and its maximum capacity is $88$ computers. Let $C(t)$ be the number of computers, where $t\\ge 0$ is the time in months. Its population obeys an inhibited growth model. The rate of increase equals $2$ times the difference between the maximum capacity and the current population. Which differential equation models this population?" :question/worked-solution "The rate at which the number of computers $C(t)$ in the data center changes is proportional to the difference between the maximum capacity and the current number of computers. Therefore, we have the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}C}{\\text{d}t}=k(a-C),\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ is the maximum capacity, and $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality.\n\nIn our case, $a=88$ computers, and $k=2$. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}C}{\\text{d}t}=2(88-C).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537170 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1d486b04-f058-5b0f-bf81-6de58ef21030" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}C}{\\text{d}t}=-6(141-C)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537145} {:answer/id #uuid "92134b70-81f2-514e-a9ea-7ac07ec43bee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}C}{\\text{d}t}=6(141-C)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537177} {:answer/id #uuid "97cd59c6-fe5e-5a8b-8a3e-b641e19282e0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}C}{\\text{d}t}=846-C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537181} {:answer/id #uuid "bb66c481-6441-50e6-9df5-076fb8d43fe2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}C}{\\text{d}t}=(141-C)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537186} {:answer/id #uuid "d6493761-babb-5f3f-92ed-987fa217746f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}C}{\\text{d}t}=6(34-C)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537194}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537177} :answer-field/id #uuid "fd1be51c-0b3a-58b7-b967-cde05f295d58" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537203}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "77765534-844a-550d-9d04-0aee6519495b" :question/problem "A data center initially contains $34$ computers, and its maximum capacity is $141$ computers. Let $C(t)$ be the number of computers, where $t\\ge 0$ is the time in months. Its population obeys an inhibited growth model. The rate of increase equals $6$ times the difference between the maximum capacity and the current population. Which differential equation models this population?" :question/worked-solution "The rate at which the number of computers $C(t)$ in the data center changes is proportional to the difference between the maximum capacity and the current number of computers. Therefore, we have the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}C}{\\text{d}t}=k(a-C),\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ is the maximum capacity, and $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality.\n\nIn our case, $a=141$ computers, and $k=6$. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}C}{\\text{d}t}=6(141-C).\n$$"}]}
